title: Status bar and task bar
tags: GUI
kind: reference
aliases: status-bar status task-bar spinner progress key-hints
summary: The status line says what the last command did; the task bar above it shows what is still running — daemon tasks, scripts, slow daemon calls.

!! The status bar

A read-only line at the very bottom of the window, never focused. It shows:

* the message of the last thing that reported — ''Metarecord deleted.'', a reconcile's summary — in red for an error, with a spinner while a command says it is busy;
* the name of the workspace it belongs to, and, dimmed, the last command run.

It belongs to a workspace: when the two slots show the same workspace there is one
line, when they show two there are two, each its own workspace's. Every status
message also goes to the workspace's message log, the [[message panel]].

''A message stays until the next one replaces it'', so that there is time to read it;
<<key "status:clear" ctrl+l>> (`status:clear`, which works while typing too) clears
it. The durations a message carries — `status-message-ms`, `status-error-ms` in
[[GUI configuration]], `timeout_ms` of `POST /gui/message` — no longer hide
anything.

Two bars can appear above it, so that a status message never covers them: the
continuations of a key sequence being typed, and the question a running script is
asking, with the keys it accepts.

!! Bars that float over the panels

Above the [[command input|The command input]], whatever comes and goes — the find
bar, the task bar, these two bars, and the command input's candidates — ''floats over
the bottom of the panels'' instead of pushing them up. A panel keeps its size whatever
shows or hides around it: a script's question disappears and comes back between two
answers, a task bar entry comes and goes with every task, and each made every panel
jump. The price is that a bar covers the last rows of the panels while it is shown.

!! The task bar

Above the command input, and only while there is something in it, the task bar shows
what is in flight for the workspaces on screen: a daemon task shows for the
repositories those workspaces have open, a script for the workspaces it owns.

* ''Daemon tasks'' — every pending or running task of the repositories the visible workspaces have open, whoever started it, the command line included. A task of another repository is not shown: only where its repository is in use do actions wait behind it, and a workspace on another repository works normally (the [[repos panel]] and `mf task list --all` still list every one). Each is drawn as a bar when the task knows its amount of work, a spinner otherwise, labelled with its kind and phase ([[Following a task]]). The GUI asks the daemon every 300 ms, and shows a task only once it has run for a second: a watcher flush lasts milliseconds and fires whenever a watched file is touched, and showing it would flash a bar for work nobody asked to see — what a flush did goes to the message log instead ([[What a flush reports]]).
* ''Scripts'' — a `!` command, a `script:run` script or a `file:open-with` program, for as long as it runs, labelled by its name. It spins while working, and gains a progress bar when the script reports its counts through [[mf gui progress]], with a phase naming the current step. While the script waits for an answer it stops spinning and says ''your answer'': working and waiting for you must never look alike. A script's entry belongs to the workspaces the script owns, and is hidden with them ([[Script sessions]]).
* ''A daemon call you are waiting on'' — a request this GUI sent and whose answer has not come back yet; see below for how it differs from a daemon task.

!! A daemon call you are waiting on

Most daemon calls — a write, a query — take milliseconds and show nothing. A few do
not, and while one runs every other action on the repository waits behind it:
without anything on screen the GUI does not look busy but broken — keys do nothing,
the panels do not move, and nothing tells that from a crash.

So the task bar shows one entry for the daemon calls made on your behalf that are
still running, labelled `<METHOD> <path>` with a spinner, and `+N` when others wait
behind it. It appears only once a call has lasted 300 ms, so the fast majority never
make it flicker, and it names the ''oldest'' call still running — the one actually
waited on. The GUI's background polls (the change feed, the task bar's own) have
nobody waiting on them and are not counted.

!!! Not the same thing as a daemon task

The two entries look alike but are seen from opposite sides:

|!|!Daemon task |!Daemon call you are waiting on |
|what it is |a unit of work in the daemon's task registry ([[Task kinds]]) |one HTTP request of ''this'' GUI, sent and not yet answered |
|who sees it |the daemon; the GUI learns of it by polling |the GUI itself, without asking the daemon |
|whose |anyone's: the command line, another GUI, the watcher — on a repository a visible workspace has open |only this GUI's own, made because you acted |
|does the GUI wait |not necessarily: an asynchronous task (a reconcile, a scan) answers its request at once and runs on |yes: until it returns, the actions behind it are stuck |
|label |the task's kind and phase, with its progress |`<METHOD> <path>`, a spinner, no progress |

They often show together, each telling half of the story:

* a reconcile started from the GUI: its `POST …/reconcile` returns at once, so there is only the ''task'' — nothing is blocked;
* a query that arrives during that reconcile: the query waits its turn ([[Task concurrency]]), so the ''call'' `POST …/query` says what is stuck, and the reconcile ''task'' says why;
* a slow query on a large repository: both — the `query` task, registered so that others see the repository is busy, and the call this GUI waits on;
* a slow write or settling call that is no task at all: only the ''call''.

